<section class="section section--line" aria-labelledby="design-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow__dot"></span>How we design it</span>
      <h2 id="design-heading">Three decisions that make or break an implementation</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="detail-grid" data-stagger>
      <article class="card detail">
        <h3>Start with the revenue motion</h3>
        <p>Before creating properties, we map how a person moves from first touch to customer and expansion, and separate the business questions you need answered from the tools that answer them. That keeps the portal from becoming a mirror of every historical process.</p>
        <ul><li>Lifecycle documented from first touch to customer</li><li>Objects defined for people, companies, deals, tickets, and custom entities</li><li>Written rules for what moves a record from one stage to the next</li></ul>
      </article>
      <article class="card detail">
        <h3>Objects, pipelines, and stages together</h3>
        <p>Objects describe what you manage, pipelines describe the process, and stages describe progress through it. We keep those layers distinct. A deal stage represents a verifiable buyer or delivery milestone, not an internal task such as “send proposal”.</p>
        <ul><li>One source of truth for each business concept</li><li>Short, observable stage names tied to exit criteria</li><li>Separate pipelines only when process, ownership, or reporting genuinely differ</li></ul>
      </article>
      <article class="card detail">
        <h3>Permissions and governance are part of the build</h3>
        <p>A scalable portal makes the safe path the easy path. We create permission sets around roles, decide who can edit sensitive properties, and publish naming conventions before teams start creating their own fields and workflows.</p>
        <ul><li>Property catalogue with owner, definition, and allowed values</li><li>Rules for required fields, sensitive data, and export access</li><li>A regular review of unused properties, workflows, and views</li></ul>
      </article>
      <article class="card detail">
        <h3>Reporting is designed, not bolted on</h3>
        <p>We start from the decisions leadership needs to make, then make sure the data needed for those dashboards is captured at the right moment by the right person. Dashboards come last because they depend on everything underneath them being trustworthy.</p>
        <ul><li>Sales, marketing, and leadership dashboards</li><li>Required properties at each stage so reports are complete</li><li>Definitions written down so everyone reads numbers the same way</li></ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
